-- title:
--   Packing a digit list into one integer: result
-- statement:
--   Let stack $E_i$ hold the reversed list $L$ of nonnegative integers, register `CR` hold $2^b$ and `CM` hold $|L|$, with $E_i$ and $X$ distinct from the scratch registers `CA`, `CY`, `CR`, `CM`, `EC` (and from each other). Then the program `packOne E_i X` consumes $L$ and pushes on $X$ the integer `kpack (2^b) L` $= \sum_k L_k 2^{bk}$.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** `kpack B L` is the base-$B$ Horner evaluation of the digit list $L$ (least significant first). The machine layer: `IState HS` is a state with $64$ stacks of items (`Item` = integer or mark), an input position and an output; `RM` programs (straight-line register code `RS`, loops `rep`, conditionals, calls of the multiplication procedure through a `MulSpec` $M$ giving its domain and cost) have a functional semantics `p.run s`, a definedness predicate `p.ok M s` and a cost `p.cost M s`; `Spec M p s s' c` abbreviates `p.ok M s ∧ p.run s = s' ∧ p.cost M s ≤ c`. `ints L` / `gints U` push an integer list / a list of pairs as items (head = top); `zsz z` is the bit length of $|z|$. `MulBnd M m Φ` says the multiplication procedure accepts all pairs of integers of bit length $\le m$ at cost $\le Φ$. Register names (`GU`, `CR`, `KB`, `XN`, …) are fixed stack indices.
-- source:
--   D. Harvey, J. van der Hoeven, Integer multiplication in time O(n log n), Ann. of Math. 193 (2021), 563–617 (Theorem 1.1: multitape Turing machine multiplication in O(n log n)). Lemma `NLogN.packOne_run` of the Lean development NLogN/HEnc.lean (lines 100–128), written for the prove2.me integer-multiplication campaign.

